        HelpEntry(
            id = "vault",
            title = "Vault",
            keywords = listOf("vault", "documents", "expiry dates", "document expiry", "document scan", "copy of a document", "keep a receipt"),
            what = "your documents and the day each runs out, reminded when the renewal window opens.",
            how = "Type the document and its expiry, e.g. \"passport 20 May 2027\"; a passport is reminded six months ahead, then a month, a week and on the day.",
            tips = listOf("Document numbers aren't kept, only the name and the date.", "Or share a photo or screenshot of the document to Navilife (or photograph it with Capture → 📷 Photo of a letter): it reads the name and expiry on the device and offers 🗂 Add to the Vault.", "Tap a document → Add a copy to keep a photo or PDF of it (1.4 MB at most). It is locked with your Vault key on the device before it leaves, so Navilife's server only holds locked bytes; Open copy shows it in the app, even offline, after your fingerprint (Touch ID on the Mac); the copies lock again when you leave the Vault or after 5 minutes.", "A receipt Inbox autopilot tidied can be kept here too: open it in Archived for you (or All tidied mail) → Keep in the Vault. It shows what it cost, when you bought it and the warranty's end when the email says, with Open the email; Take it out of the Vault on the same email undoes it.", "Your documents are locked with one Vault key kept only on your phone and Mac. Under VAULT KEY a new phone or Mac asks for it with a six-digit code: Allow on a device that has the key only if both show the same code. A new recovery key opens it too.", "Ask or tell Talk \"when does my passport run out?\", \"do I have a copy of my licence?\" or \"what's in my Vault?\": the answer comes from the Vault on this device (never the AI), with Open the Vault under it, even offline."),
            fold = HelpPlace.VAULT,
            foldWhere = "Ask → Settings → Vault",
            mac = HelpPlace.VAULT,
            macWhere = "Ask → Settings → Vault",
            macHow = null,
            data = true,
        ),
        HelpEntry(
            id = "voice-notes",
            title = "Voice notes",
            keywords = listOf("voice note", "voice notes", "voice memo", "record a note", "take a voice note", "ramble", "dictate", "dictation", "recording"),
            what = "talk a rambling note and Navilife turns it into words on the phone or the Mac, then offers the tasks, dates and reminders in it as cards for you to add. The recording is kept for 30 days.",
            how = "Open Capture (the widget, the quick-settings tile or Share) and hold the 🎙: it records until you tap Done (Pause stops it for a moment, five minutes at most). Or say \"take a voice note\" to Talk. When you stop, the words appear and Navilife reads them: Add the cards you want; anything you leave waits in Needs you.",
            tips = listOf("The recording and its words stay on the device that made it (the phone or the Mac), encrypted, and go after 30 days; tap Keep on a note in Ask → Daily → Voice notes to hold on to it. Only the words, never the recording, go to Navilife's AI.", "Turning speech into words happens on the device: Android's own recogniser on the phone (Android 13 or later), Dictation's English on the Mac. If it can't, the recording is still kept and you can play it back.", "No signal? The note is still recorded and turned into words; tap Make cards on it in Voice notes once you're back online.", "Navilife's AI tidies the note into a few short lines and says who it mentions; your own words stay under \"Your words\". Ideas with no date can go to Lists → Someday → Ideas with one tap.", "A quick tap on the 🎙 is the short way: say one thing (\"buy milk tomorrow\", \"add eggs to the shopping\"); your words show in the field as you speak and, about a second after you stop, it is added, with Undo and Edit for a few seconds (tap the 🎙 again to finish sooner). With \"Add what you ask for\" on Ask first (What Navilife may do), it fills the capture field for your ↑ Add instead.", "On the Galaxy Watch, tap 🎙 Voice note under Capture (or say \"take a voice note\" to Capture) and talk until you tap Done: the watch writes the words down itself (nothing is recorded), Navilife tidies them and the cards wait in Needs you on the phone. With no signal the words are kept as a task."),
            fold = HelpPlace.VOICE_NOTES,
            foldWhere = "Ask → Daily → Voice notes",
            mac = HelpPlace.VOICE_NOTES,
            macWhere = "Ask → Daily → Voice notes",
            macHow = "On the Mac hold the mic at the end of Today's capture field (a click still talks), or say \"take a voice note\" to Talk: the Mac records it and turns it into words itself, keeps it encrypted on the Mac for 30 days, and its cards wait in Needs you.",
        ),
        HelpEntry(
            id = "voice",
            title = "Obi's voice",
            keywords = listOf("obis voice", "obi's voice", "adas voice", "navilifes voice", "robotic voice", "voice sounds", "change voice", "spoken voice"),
            what = "how Obi, Navilife's assistant, sounds in Talk, the morning brief and the call assistant.",
            how = "Pick a voice there and play a sample.",
            tips = listOf("On a slow connection the first words can take a moment; offline it says \"Using the phone's voice while offline\" once and carries on in the phone's own voice.", "Obi talks a touch quicker than before, with a small natural pause after a quick reaction (\"Oh nice.\"), and each sentence of his answer is made ready as he writes it, so there are no gaps between them (Amazon voices; Google's voices keep their own pace).", "Ask → More → Activity shows how quickly the voice answered; clips heard over Bluetooth or car audio are marked \"+ Bluetooth\", about 0.2 s more.", "In Talk the first words of an answer come with the answer itself, so Navilife starts speaking without a second wait; Activity marks those \"with the answer\".", "At the top, Obi calls you lets you choose whether it calls you by your name or \"sir\" in Ask, Talk and Hello; the choice follows you to every device.", "Under it, What Obi remembers lists a line about each proper chat (kept 30 days in your own data) so Ask, Talk and Hello can pick up where you left off; choose Don't remember to start fresh each time (it forgets them too), or Forget all.", "At the foot of Obi's voice, Speed shows your last 20 hellos and spoken answers: how long each took to start (aim: a hello within 0.6 s of your tap, an answer within 1.5 s of you finishing) and where the wait went (sending, the answer, the voice). It is kept on this device only and holds times, never what was said; Clear empties it.", "Under Speed, On the server shows how long Navilife's server took over the last day's answers, quick look-ups and conversation apart: the middle time to the first words, the first sentence (aim: within 1 second), the voice ready and the whole answer. Times only, never what was said.", "Spoken answers, and every quick look-up, come from Obi's quicker model so his first sentence starts sooner; a typed open question (advice, planning, a long one) still gets the larger, more thoughtful one. His long standing instructions and the conversation so far are reused from one turn to the next rather than read afresh, which is quicker and costs less.", "A tap on the orb speaks at once: the hello is made ahead in Obi's voice and kept ready on this device (for this part of the day and the next hour), and while the first sentence of an answer plays the rest is already on its way, so there is no pause between them.", "While Obi works out an answer he never says \"One sec\" or any other holding line: the orb shows Thinking at once, and only if an answer takes more than about two and a half seconds does one soft, breath-like tone play. Most answers start well before that, so there is just the orb and then his voice.", "The moment you tap the orb, open Talk or start speaking, Navilife's server gets its line to the AI and to Obi's voice ready, so the first answer doesn't wait for a fresh connection. Nothing you say is sent for this, and it costs nothing.", "On the phone, a quick question that only looks something up (\"what's on tomorrow?\", \"when's the bin day?\") goes off the moment Navilife hears you finish, while the phone is still writing down your last words; if those words turn out different, it asks again with them. Anything that adds, moves, sends or pays, chats and Money questions always wait for your final words.", "With headphones (AirPods, a Bluetooth headset, the car) you can answer the hello while it is still speaking: the microphone opens with it, ignores Navilife's own voice and stops the hello once you say a couple of words, so none of yours are lost. On the speaker, talking over the hello stops it a little quicker than other lines.", "Obi's voice → Live (off until you turn it on) lets you talk with Obi in real time on Google's Gemini Live: no waiting for his turn, and you can talk over him any time. Starting Talk opens a Live conversation; tap the orb to end it. Pick his Live voice under the switch.", "If Live can't open within two seconds (no signal, the month's £15 used up, its key not set up yet), Obi answers as he normally does. While Live is on your voice and today's Navilife context go to Google; Navilife keeps no recordings, only how many seconds were heard and spoken.", "Under Obi's Live voice each voice has ▶ Sample: it plays the start of today's brief in Google's studio voice of the same name, which Live's voice is built on. If Google's voices can't be reached it says so rather than playing a different voice.", "In Live, Obi starts each talk knowing your day and your name, and looks up anything current (news, scores, travel) with the web search Ask uses; a search about your own life or family is never sent. Each answer shows in Ask with what he said and where it came from.", "In Live, Obi can look at another day himself (\"what's on Thursday?\") from your calendar and tasks on the phone, and offer a change (\"add a task\", \"move the dentist to four\") as Ask's usual card: nothing happens until you tap it, and he can never send, pay, book or trade.", "With Live on, \"Hello, Navilife\" (the orb, the phrase, or the bedside clock's orb) still greets you at once, then Obi carries on live: just answer him. A long Live talk keeps going past ten minutes while you're talking (up to about an hour); after two quiet minutes it ends at the next break.", "In Live, Obi can check your email (\"anything from the school?\") from the mail on your phone, and answer money questions (\"what did I spend on takeaways?\") from Money while it is unlocked; locked, he says so and Ask shows Unlock. Emails are other people's words, so he never acts on one by himself.", "On the Mac, Live is the same switch and voices under Obi's voice, and clicking Talk's orb opens a Live talk (click it again to end). The Mac's echo canceller keeps his own voice out of the microphone on the speakers. With Live on, the Mac's hello (clicking the orb at rest, or saying \"Hello Obi\") still greets you at once, then Obi carries on live: just answer him, or click the orb to end."),
            fold = HelpPlace.VOICE,
            foldWhere = "Ask → Settings → Obi's voice",
            mac = HelpPlace.VOICE,
            macWhere = "Ask → Settings → Obi's voice",
            macHow = null,
        ),
        HelpEntry(
            id = "watch",
            title = "Galaxy Watch",
            keywords = listOf("watch app", "my watch", "wear os", "watch tile", "complication"),
            what = "Navilife on your Galaxy Watch: Up next with Done, quick capture by voice, a tile and a complication.",
            how = "Open Navilife on the watch and enter the code it shows under Settings → Watch.",
            tips = listOf("The watch syncs on its own, it isn't a remote screen for the phone.", "On a Barça match day the watch shows the match from an hour before kick-off (when and where it's on), then the live score and the last goal, then full time; the tile says the same in one line and, while it's on, the complication's ring is the score. Each goal's buzz is the phone's heads-up on your wrist.", "Before a meeting with other people the watch shows the prep too, from the lead time you set in Mail until ten minutes after the start: when it starts, who's coming and the one or two most useful lines (their last email, what's open, when you last met); the tile says it in one line.", "With no signal the watch keeps what you tap (Done, captures, a fast) and sends it when it's back online; the foot of its screen says \"Offline · your changes here wait to sync\" and the tile says \"Waiting to sync\" until they've gone.", "To unlink the watch from the watch itself, scroll to the foot of its screen and tap Unlink this watch, then Unlink: it needs a connection, sends what's waiting first, then forgets your day and shows a new code. You can also unlink it from Settings → Watch on the phone or the Mac.", "The watch keeps Navilife's quiet hours (Settings → Notifications): its screen and tile say \"Quiet hours · until 07:00\", the tile leaves out the inbox count till morning, and to save battery it syncs about hourly and refreshes less often overnight. A parking timer, a meeting about to start or a live match still count down as usual.", "When Navilife drafts a reply to a WhatsApp or text message, the watch shows it under MESSAGE REPLY. Send has your phone send it through the message's own Reply, so it works only while the message is still in your phone's notifications; otherwise it says \"Needs your phone\" and the draft stays. Nothing goes without your tap; edit it or choose Not now on your phone or the Mac.", "On an office day's commute the watch shows when to leave: \"Leave by 07:32\" for your train from the live board, else \"Leave about 07:40\" from your commute in Work mode, counting down the last hour; it turns brass when a train is cancelled or late, or ten minutes before. On the train it says how long to your change; in the evening when you'll be home.", "Under Up next the watch lists TODAY's next three tasks in the order you gave them on the Board, any carried from an earlier day first (their line in brass), with \"2 done · 5 to go\" above and \"+4 more on your phone\" under them. Tap a task's ring to tick it off; \"Done · …\" with Undo stays for five seconds."),
            fold = HelpPlace.WATCH,
            foldWhere = "Ask → Settings → Watch",
            mac = HelpPlace.WATCH,
            macWhere = "Ask → Settings → Watch",
            macHow = null,
        ),
        HelpEntry(
            id = "week-ahead",
            title = "Week ahead",
            keywords = listOf("week ahead", "the coming week", "sunday review", "next week"),
            what = "the coming week day by day: events, office days, clashes, family, bins and parcels.",
            how = "It comes on Sunday at 18:00, or open it any time; Listen reads it aloud.",
            tips = listOf("Under the summary a Balance line says how the week looks as planned against your Balance targets (Review → Balance → Targets ›): which of family, health and your own time fall short, and whether work runs over your hours. Listen reads it too."),
            fold = HelpPlace.WEEK_AHEAD,
            foldWhere = "Ask → Daily → Week ahead",
            mac = HelpPlace.WEEK_AHEAD,
            macWhere = "Ask → Daily → Week ahead",
            macHow = null,
        ),
        HelpEntry(
            id = "week-board",
            title = "Week board",
            keywords = listOf("week board", "day timeline", "today timeline", "hour by hour", "timeline tab", "hide timeline", "card area", "area dot", "colour categories", "category dot", "side hustle list", "house list", "weekend", "saturday and sunday", "five columns", "monday to friday", "lists under the week", "add a task to a day", "quick add", "tick off", "untick", "drag and drop", "reorder by hand", "board", "board view", "plan the week", "move a task to another day", "drag tasks", "reorder tasks", "kanban", "week view of tasks", "time estimate", "how long", "planned", "free time", "capacity", "over-planned", "no day yet", "this week", "park a task", "someday tray", "block time", "time block", "add a task to my calendar", "focus time", "goals this week", "week's goals", "tag a task to a goal", "goal progress", "stacking up", "busy day", "too much on", "pressure warning", "overloaded day", "email to a day", "add to week", "discord post to a day", "overdue to a day", "due today to another day", "needs you to a day", "school date to a day", "school trip task", "birthday gift task", "gift to a day"),
            what = "your tasks laid out a column per day, a week at a time for five weeks: each day has its big date and weekday (today underlined in gold), \"2 of 5 done\" over a thin gold bar, its events as slim chips (\"09:30 · Dentist\"), its free time as one quiet line, and its tasks as planner cards: the time and how long (\"0:30\") on top, the title, and the tick at the foot with its area (\"● Family\").",
            how = "Open Calendar and tap Board at the top. Hold a card still for a moment until it lifts, then drag it to another day, or up and down in its day: the other cards slide apart with a gold line where it will land, and the board scrolls along near an edge; let go and it lands there, with Undo on the bar. Tap a card for Move up, Move down and the next days instead.",
            tips = listOf("A card keeps its time when it moves to another day, and nothing can be moved into the past; Today's column holds tasks with no day, and ones carried over say so in gold.", "Your order is kept for each day and synced between the Fold and the Mac. \"+ Add a task\" at the top of every day opens a field with the keyboard up: type, press Return and it is added last in that day, with the field still open for the next one; tap 15m, 30m, 1h or 2h under it first to give it an estimate, and tap anywhere else to close it.", "Tap the circle at the foot of a card to tick it off: it fills gold, the card fades (the words stay plain, nothing is struck through), and a moment later it settles below the open ones in the same day (one ticked off early stays on the day it was planned for). Tap it again to un-tick it, with Undo on the bar.", "Today ticks the same way: tick a task and it stays put, struck through, for a moment, then settles into \"3 done today\" at the foot of Today, still ticked. Tap its circle to un-tick it (Undo ticks it again).", "Tap a card (right-click on the Mac) and pick How long? — 15m, 30m, 1h or 2h; Navilife marks a guess from similar tasks as suggested, but nothing is set until you pick. Each day from today then shows a pill under its date, \"planned · 4h 05 / 3h\" (against its free time: 09:00 to 21:00 less events, work and the commute), its thin bar amber when the day is over-planned.", "On the unfolded Fold and the Mac, Monday to Friday sit across with Saturday and Sunday as two shorter columns underneath, then, under a thin line, the Someday, Side hustle, House and No day yet lists side by side, each its name and how many cards it holds; the cover screen shows one day at a time.", "Each card wears a coloured dot for the part of your life it belongs to: Family (orange), Work (blue), Side hustle (brass), Money (green) or House (violet). Navilife guesses from the title (\"Boiler service\" is House, \"Send the quote\" Side hustle); tap a card (right-click on the Mac) and pick one under Area, or No area, to set it, with Undo.", "Under the weekend, Someday splits into three lists: Someday, Side hustle and House. Drag a card onto Side hustle or House to keep it there for later (it leaves its day), or onto Someday to take it off those two; each move has Undo.", "A card in one of those lists is just its title, its tick and its area; it shows a time or a length on top only when it has one. On the timeline, a task's block wears its area's colour (Work blue, Family orange, House violet), events their calendar's, the commute orange.", "Under the week's title Obi says one line about the week shown: \"Mon is 1h over · move “Gym” to Fri?\" when a day has more planned than free time (amber), \"Thu is stacking up · …\" when too much lands on one day, \"3 tasks still need a day\", or \"Everything fits this week\". When the line offers a move, tap it (click on the Mac) and that card goes there, with Undo on the bar.", "Beside the week, today shows as a timeline, hour by hour from 07:00 to 22:00: timed events in their calendar's colour, tasks planned at a time in gold for as long as they take, the commute in orange, and a gold line at now. Tap an event there to open it.", "Timeline or Hide timeline in the header opens or shuts it (on the unfolded Fold it starts shut, so the five days keep their width). On the cover screen, tap Timeline under the days to see the day you're on hour by hour; swipe across it or tap a day to step the day, and Cards takes you back.", "On the unfolded Fold and the Mac, ‹ › step a week (the week slides across); while you hold a card, the week scrolls down near the bottom edge so you can drop it on the weekend or a list (to move a card to another week, tap it, or right-click on the Mac, and pick a day).", "On the cover screen, above the day, the small chip \"No day yet · 3\" holds tasks you want to do this week but not on a set day: drag a card onto it (or pick No day this week from its menu) and it leaves Today until you drag it onto a day; ones left from an earlier week wait there saying so.", "On the cover screen, tap the No day yet chip to open its cards in a sheet from the bottom and drag one out onto a day's pill; tap it again to close it. On the unfolded Fold and the Mac, No day yet and Someday are always-open lists under the weekend: drag a card onto either, or out of one onto a day.", "On the cover screen the Someday chip beside it (\"Someday · 12\") keeps your Someday tasks folded away the same way: drag a card onto it to keep it for later, or tap it open and drag one out onto a day.", "On the unfolded Fold and the Mac, days already gone fold into narrow strips with their date and how many were done (\"3 done\"), so the rest of the week gets the room; tap one to open it, and tap its date to fold it again.", "Today follows the order you set on the Board's Today column: the tasks with no time you placed come first in your order (the top one is Up next when nothing is planned at a time), then the rest as before.", "To block time for a task in your calendar, tap its card (right-click on the Mac) and pick a start under Block time: the free times that day for its estimate (30 minutes if it has none). The task is planned then and its block is added to your Google or Outlook calendar as busy time, only when you tap, with Undo on the bar; a task that already has a block moves it instead.", "To block time by dragging (Fold unfolded, or the Mac), hold a card and drop it on one of a day's free times (\"10:00–12:00 · 2h free\"): it is blocked where you let go, on the quarter hour, moved to that day if it was elsewhere, and its block goes to your Google or Outlook calendar as busy time with Undo on the bar. A free time too short for the task says so and nothing moves.", "Across the top of the Board are the week's goals as small gold pills (\"◎ Run 5k  2/4\", filling gold as its tasks are done), up to three: \"+ Goal\" picks one of your open goals for that week or makes a new one; this week with none picked shows the same goals as Today's \"This week\" line. Tap a goal (right-click on the Mac) for Off this week.", "Drop a card on a goal (or pick it under For a goal in the card's menu) and the card says \"◎ Run 5k\"; each goal shows how many of that week's tasks for it are done, like \"2 of 4 done\". Every change has Undo.", "When three or more things land on one day (tasks due, bills and renewals, tasks carried over, evening events), that day's column wears an amber edge and says \"Stacking up: Trip form, Council tax and Gym\". One tap under it moves one of its tasks to a lighter day or does one of its due tasks today, with Undo.", "Within two days, the same \"Friday is stacking up\" line shows on Today and in the morning brief, with the same taps, and the orb mentions it when you say hello.", "To put an email on your week, open ⋯ More on it in From your mail and tap To a day…, then a day (Today, Tomorrow, the rest of this week, or next week's): it becomes a task last on that day's column, named from the subject, and the email is archived at the next check, with Undo on the bar.", "To put a Discord post on your week, tap To a day… under it in Needs you (beside Keep as idea), then a day: it becomes a task to look at it, last on that day's column, and the post reads \"On your week\". A note only, nothing is traded, with Undo on the bar.", "To give an overdue or due-today task a later day, tap To a day… under its card in Needs you, then a day: it leaves Needs you and sits last on that day's Board column, its due date kept (the line says \"still due …\"), with Undo on the bar.", "To get ready for a school date (a trip, a non-uniform day) or a birthday, tap To a day… under it in School, or in the opened birthday in Gifts, then a day up to the date itself: it becomes a task on that day's column (\"Ready for Trip to the zoo · Fri 20 Nov\" or \"Gift for … · Sat 31 Oct\", with the order-by day and your ideas in its notes), once per date, with Undo. Nothing is ordered.", "To plan your week, tap Plan your week above the Board (⇧⌘P on the Mac): what's left from last week, what's fixed each day, the week's goals, then a suggested spread that keeps office evenings light and never goes past a due day. Change any day or pick Leave; nothing moves until Accept plan, with one Undo. On Sunday it plans next week.", "On Sunday evening (at your week-ahead time, until Monday noon) Needs you shows Plan next week when something is left to place: Plan now opens the same flow, Later sets it aside, and once a plan is accepted on any device it goes. Saying \"plan my week\" to Obi opens it too; typed in Ask you get the suggested spread and where to accept it.", "From Friday, Review the week above this week's Board opens Review with the reflection open: under the week's facts it says how much of what you planned on the Board got done (\"5 of 8 planned done · best day Tue, 3 done\") and what kept slipping (tasks carried two days or more, or parked since last week), which also lead the What to drop suggestions.", "What you write as the one thing that matters next week is carried into Plan your week: its first step starts \"From your review · Run 5k\"."),
